{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And if your brother grows poor, etc.</b> This passage will awaken a person concerning the spirit of HaShem, our brother, which dwells within us. And it says ״כִּי יָמוּךְ אָחִיךָ״ — this is the spirit of life which He planted within us to give us life. If a person sees that it has not illuminated in Torah and not in commandments — for there is no poverty except from Torah and the commandments — <b>and his hand falters</b>, and he darkens his light and his splendor, <b>with you</b> — meaning, on account of being with you it is that he collapsed, for outside of you his light shone from one end of the world to the other. And you will find that HaShem seeks to return the spirit to him as He gave it, as it says (Ecclesiastes 12:7) ״וְהָרוּחַ תָּשׁוּב״, etc., and they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 152b): ״Give it back to Him as He gave it to you.״ And HaShem commands <b>and you shall strengthen him</b> — meaning, that he should strengthen himself to return in repentance, to restore him to his place; for there is nothing that establishes the vital spirit in a high place like repentance." "<b>And if your brother grows poor and his hand falters with you.</b> Know that those who know the hidden things of HaShem (Sefer ha-Yashar, chapter 32) said that there is a category of souls that HaShem brings into a person through the sod of ibbur for known reasons. Some come to strive with a person when he performs some commandment, in order to participate in the deed, and thereby merit. And some come and settle for wondrous reasons that HaShem has marvelously arranged in the rectification of souls. Corresponding to both of them it says <b>ger and toshav and he shall live with you</b> — corresponding to the two souls that come without his knowledge when he strengthens himself to return in repentance. And he must further beware lest he darken the light of his spirit and of the souls that sojourn with him; this is what it says <b>do not take from him</b> — what is in him. And by what means? By נשך and מרבית, as I shall explain below. <b>And you shall fear your Elohim</b> — meaning, for from the quarry of pure souls He returned his deposit to him, and He further seeks <b>and your brother shall live with you</b> — that he should restore the soul with additions of vitality through the agency of holy service that HaShem has commanded him. And although they said 'give it back as it was given to you,' that is the aspect of return; or, according to what I explained in my book Chefetz HaShem (Shabbat 152b), that when one gives it to HaShem as He gave it to him, thereby it is elevated and adds life and supernal light — and see there."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {1 "The phrase 'בְּסִפְרִי חֵפֶץ ה׳' refers to the Or HaChaim's own work; the parenthetical source reference (Shabbat 152b) clarifies the Talmudic passage under discussion. The bracketed correction [מַחְצָב] over (מחלב) follows the apparent intention; translated as 'quarry.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:33.244452308Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}